lesson g6-negative-numbers v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=1 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Negative numbers: distance from zero, and calculating across zero"
  goal "Add and take away positive and negative numbers, tell a number's size from its distance from zero, and find a first product with a negative number by carrying a pattern past zero."

  look {
    scene 32x16 {
      thermometer th from=-15 to=15 step=5 value=-6 unit="°C" at=canvas(1, 1)
      midnightsun sky suns=4 midnight=1 right-of=th gap=2
    }
    say """
      On the ice shelf the sun never sets in summer, but the air can still be below zero. A
      thermometer is a number line stood on end. Adding a negative number moves down it, like a fall
      in temperature: -6 + (-4) = -10. Taking away a negative number moves up it, the same as adding
      the positive one: -6 - (-4) = -2. The distance of a number from zero is written with bars,
      |-6| = 6, so -6 is smaller than 4 but further from zero. The difference between two readings
      is the higher one take away the lower one.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked negative.cliff-colder h=3 c=-14 level=medium
    }
    practice negative.cliff-colder count=1 seed=5
    practice negative.whale-dive count=1 seed=4
    practice negative.rule-sums count=1 seed=6
    practice negative.shore-distance count=1 seed=3
    show negative.times-pattern v=0 n=4 m=3 easy-n=2 easy-m=2 hard-v=1 hard-n=3 hard-m=3
    show negative.rule-sums a=-6 b=-7 op="+" easy-a=5 easy-b=-9 hard-a=-6 hard-b=-9 hard-op="-"
    practice negative.start-before count=1 seed=7
    level hard {
      practice negative.shore-distance count=1 seed=11
    }
    practice negative.always-or-never count=1 seed=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show negative.leo-minus-negative a=3 b=-8 easy-b=-4 hard-a=-5 hard-b=-11
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show negative.magic-ice s=-2 easy-s=2 hard-s=-4
  }
  remember {
    say "Adding a negative number moves down the scale. Taking away a negative number moves up it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Keep a thermometer or a number line in view the whole time: every question here is a move up or
    down it. The common slips are treating 3 - (-8) as 3 - 8, and adding the two sizes when working
    out the difference between -4 and -17, so ask each time which way the answer moves and whether
    it crosses zero. The shore question separates two ideas children mix up: -8 is smaller than 5,
    but further from zero, and the bars |-8| mean the distance only. The logbook pattern is the
    first multiplication with a negative number: the answers keep falling by the same step past
    zero, which is why a negative times a positive is negative. In the start question the day is
    undone backwards, the rise first. In the two-star question Leo took away 8 instead of adding it.
    In the three-star question the nine numbers add to nine times the middle one, and three rows
    share that equally. If the signs still get muddled, stay here another day with the thermometer
    and small numbers. Off the page, read the temperature in the freezer and the fridge and work out
    how many degrees apart they are.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a difference across zero is worked once in the teacher's pen, the differences start above zero, the whale rises only 5 m, the calculations add a negative, the puffin on the shore is east of the hut, the pattern reaches (-2) × 2, the midnight reading starts above zero, the claim is the plainest, Leo's mistake is with -4, and the magic square runs from -2 to 6."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level both readings in each difference are below zero, the whale rises back further than it started, the calculations take away a negative from a negative, both animals on the shore can be west of the hut, the pattern goes on to a negative times a negative, the midnight question undoes a rise of 10, the claim needs a counterexample, Leo's mistake starts below zero, and the magic square runs from -8 to 0."
  }
}
